{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_KXcLPtepRN0AyCxHohofVGON","content":"1\t# xlang slice 2 findings\n2\t\n3\t## Implemented\n4\t\n5\t- Generalized `yang.call.conformance` with frontend profiles for TypeScript, PHP, and Python. Publication, runtime initialization, donor execution, export reads, caller projection, linking, and rendering all use the shared profile path.\n6\t- Added Python <-> TypeScript and Python <-> PHP in both directions. Every new leg passes a callback into the foreign function, stores it, checks same-cell identity, calls it back, and asserts the result.\n7\t- Python integers are bigint values at the JavaScript boundary. TypeScript uses bigint arguments and arithmetic when calling Python; the reverse callback converts its bigint argument with `Number`, and the Python donor uses `float` before adding a JavaScript-compatible number. PHP's integer path needs no conversion.\n8\t- Python `print` caller sources include the parser-required final newline.\n9\t\n10\t## Named skip\n11\t\n12\t- `yang.clojure <-> TypeScript`, both directions: `yang.clojure` has convention-wired application lowering, but no published runtime module, `run-main` entry, or runtime global namespace through which the shared helper can execute a donor and project exports. Supporting these legs requires frontend/runtime work rather than a minimal test-helper generalization.\n13\t\n14\t## Gates\n15\t\n16\t- `clojure -M:test -n yang.cross-lang-test`: pending final run.\n17\t- `clojure -M:kondo --lint test/yang/call/conformance.clj test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj`: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n18\t- `mise exec -- cljstyle check test/yang/call/conformance.clj test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj`: passed."}
